          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease (CAD) is a build-up of atherosclerotic plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. The plaque forms over decades. It starts with injury to the inner lining of the artery, followed by the gradual buildup of cholesterol-rich material beneath that lining. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.)\n\nTwo different processes can cause symptoms:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing.** As the artery narrows, it can't supply enough blood when the heart works harder. This usually causes predictable chest discomfort during exertion, called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture.** A plaque can crack, exposing material that triggers a blood clot. The clot can block the artery suddenly, causing an acute coronary syndrome, such as a heart attack. This can happen at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms. A normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now. (Source: *Coronary artery disease*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe sources I searched do not describe the stages of plaque growth in detail, so I can't give a more specific timeline. Guidance on estimating heart and blood-vessel risk uses age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, blood pressure treatment, diabetes and smoking status. (Source: *Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*, cardiology society guidance.) A clinician can explain how these factors apply to a specific person.\n\nIf you or someone else has chest pain, pressure, or breathlessness, especially if it is new, worsening, or at rest, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
